<p>Google has added new testing and planning features around AI Max for Search campaigns. In an August 20, 2026 update, Google described testing different budgets and ROI targets across multiple Search campaigns in a single A/B test, new controls for brand and location settings, and expanded Performance Planner suggestions. The important SEO and PPC lesson is to test the change against a baseline instead of treating AI automation as an automatic win.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/google-ai-max-testing-planning-tools-2026.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="Google AI Max campaign testing workflow from baseline to experiment and decision"></figure>
<h2>What the new tools are for</h2><p>Google’s update focuses on measurement and planning rather than simply adding another campaign setting. The testing tools are meant to help advertisers compare different budgets or ROI targets, while Performance Planner can model changes such as bidding or budget targets against existing campaign performance.</p>
<h2>Use a clean baseline first</h2><p>Before testing, record the campaign’s current spend, conversions, conversion value, CPA or ROAS, impression share where relevant, brand traffic, location performance and major business constraints. Keep the baseline period long enough to reduce noise from normal day-to-day changes.</p>
<h2>Test one meaningful change at a time</h2><p>If the question is whether a larger budget improves business output, make the budget change the main test variable. If the question is whether a different ROI target changes volume, isolate that variable. Mixing many changes at once makes the result harder to explain.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What to record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Baseline</td><td>Spend, conversions, value and business constraints</td></tr><tr><td>Experiment</td><td>Budget/target change and guardrails</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Incremental results and efficiency</td></tr><tr><td>Decision</td><td>Keep, reject or run another test</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Keep brand and location controls visible</h2><p>Google says new AI Max experiment capabilities can test with specific brand or location controls enabled. These settings can be important when a campaign serves a defined geographic market or when branded demand behaves differently from non-brand demand. Document these controls before the test so a later result is not misread as a pure automation effect.</p>
<h2>Use Performance Planner as a planning input</h2><p>Performance Planner can help estimate how changes such as bidding or budget targets may affect campaign performance. Treat these outputs as planning scenarios, not guaranteed results. Actual performance can differ because auctions, demand, competition, landing pages and conversion rates change.</p>
<h2>Measure business impact, not only platform metrics</h2><p>A campaign can improve a platform metric while failing a business goal. Where possible, connect the experiment to qualified leads, revenue, margin or another real outcome. If those signals are not available, state the limitation clearly rather than presenting a proxy metric as proof of business growth.</p>
<h2>When an AI Max test should not be rushed</h2><p>Do not force an experiment during a period with major tracking changes, site migrations, unusual promotions or large demand shocks. First make sure conversion measurement is stable. Otherwise, the test can measure a tracking or market change instead of the campaign setting you intended to study.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Does Google guarantee that AI Max will improve performance?</h3><p>No. Google provides testing and planning tools, but campaign results depend on the account, market, settings, demand and measurement quality.</p><h3>Can brand and location controls be included in tests?</h3><p>Google says newer AI Max experiment capabilities support tests with specific brand or location settings enabled.</p>
